var e=`# CHAPTER 10 — The Cake

I came back from the kitchen fighting with one hand and holding the plate with the other.

Jab. Dodge. Balance the cake. Knee. Balance the cake. A guy tried to tackle me around the waist, and I spun with the plate held high, like a waiter at a fancy restaurant. Not one strawberry fell.

From outside came the first sound of a siren.

---

Kim saw me.

He saw the plate. He saw the cake. And something changed in his face that I'd only ever seen before in a serious fight.

He left the table. For the first time all night, he left the table.

And he started coming toward me, clearing a path. One guy to the left, another to the right. Me heading toward him, swinging on my side, plate held high. Two brothers meeting in the middle of a war, over a slice of cake.

Ten feet. Six.

Somebody kicked my hand.

---

The cake flew.

I saw it in slow motion. The plate spinning one way, the slice of cake the other, the strawberry coming loose in the air.

Kim stopped. He just watched the cake rise. Didn't blink.

And Freddy, who'd been behind Kim all night, saw Kim frozen with his fist half closed and his eyes locked. He figured his moment had finally come. He wound up the punch of his life, arm way back, and went all in.

---

Kim crouched down to catch the cake.

He spun 180 degrees on the floor, palm open, and the cake landed right in his hand. Not even the frosting got squished.

Freddy's punch went through the air where Kim's head had been half a second earlier. His body followed the punch, and Freddy tripped over Kim, who was still crouched.

On the other side was a guy in the middle of a kick, aiming for Kim's mouth down on the floor. He tried to adjust and it turned into a knee.

It caught Freddy square in the middle of his face.

The sound was horrible. Freddy dropped stiff, and his eye had already started swelling before he hit the ground.

---

Kim stood up, took a bite of the cake and closed his eyes.

— Worth it.

I was on my knees next to Freddy.

— No. No, no, no, no. I tried. I tried to protect him, goddammit. All night long. Oh my God. Son of a bitch.

— You're the one who said not to touch him — Kim said, mouth full.

— I know!

— I didn't touch him.

— I KNOW, KIM!

---

The siren reached the front door.

We went out the back, through the same loading dock we'd come in by. Kim finished the cake on the way. I left with the bottle under my arm, because I'd said the party was going to end and I'm not one to blow a prediction.

Behind us, more than thirty people laid out on the floor of a rich kid's house, and a blond guy with a black eye who has no idea he was the only one in there we protected.

---

So that's it.

That black eye you saw on Tuesday came from a knee that wasn't ours. We didn't touch Freddy. Not me, not the side character. I've got witnesses: half of Marélia was in that room.

We are innocent until proven guilty.

And nobody ever proved a thing.
`;export{e as default};
